# November 28, 2026: state hospitals and asylums of Kentucky, Tennessee, North Carolina and South Carolina, a batch of the
# themed pass on historic state hospitals. No asylum in these four states was on the map before.
# Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia's articles on each institution and its list of Kirkbride Plan hospitals; the
# Park Service's National Register records and forms (Western Lunatic Asylum 79003612, Central Kentucky Lunatic Asylum
# 83002646, Western North Carolina Insane Asylum 77000996, Western State Hospital Historic District 87001057, Dix Hill
# 90001638, the Mills Building 70000890 and the Babcock Building 81000570) and the North Carolina office's copies of the
# Broughton and Dix Hill forms; the Park Service's Places pages; SAH Archipedia; Kentucky Historic Institutions
# (kyhi.org); the Kentucky Historical Society's and Tennessee's and North Carolina's marker texts and HMdb; the
# Knoxville History Project and the City of Knoxville; the City of Raleigh and Dix Park; the North Carolina health
# department's Caswell history; the UNC School of Government's Broughton reuse report; Historic Columbia; Bluegrass
# Community and Technical College and its builder and architects; Abandoned Southeast. Asylum Projects was used only
# where the text says so. No patient is named. Pins: OpenStreetMap features, address lookups and marker points, every
# one looked at on the aerial.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'asylum_ktnc_gwqj','label':'State hospitals and asylums, Kentucky, Tennessee and the Carolinas','added':'2026-11-28 07:48'}
def NR(n,t): return ['National Register record: '+t+' ('+n+')','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/'+n]
KYHI='https://kyhi.org/asylums/'
KBL=W('Kirkbride Plan')
PLACES=[
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Kentucky
N('eastern-state-hospital-lexington-ky','Eastern State Hospital (the Lunatic Asylum), old administration building','Lexington','ky','inst','standing',38.06174,-84.4986,'site','view','walk','','1824–2013',
  "Kentucky's first asylum and, by Wikipedia's count, the second-oldest psychiatric hospital in the country. Lexington citizens began a Fayette Hospital in 1816, and Henry Clay spoke at its dedication in 1817, but it was never finished. The legislature passed an act to establish a lunatic asylum in December 1822 (the state marker says December 4, Wikipedia December 7), bought the unfinished building with ten acres, and took the first patient on May 1, 1824. The main building grew round that first one between 1835 and 1870, and the population reached 2,000 by 1945. The hospital moved to a new building on Bull Lea Road in September 2013, the old grounds went to Bluegrass Community and Technical College, and the old asylum buildings were pulled down by 2014.",
  "Only two old buildings were kept: the administration building and the laundry. The laundry, built in 1906, is now the college's student centre, The Den, with its brick walls and steel roof trusses left open inside. The administration building, more than a hundred years old according to its builder, is the college's admissions and student services office and has been renovated with a new addition, due to open in spring or summer 2026. When it was built, and of what, was not found. The hospital cemetery was moved at least twice and since 1984 has been behind the Hope Center; Wikipedia says the state counts about 4,400 burials, and Kentucky Historic Institutions estimates many more.",
  '','The Newtown campus of Bluegrass Community and Technical College, 500 Newtown Pike, where the state marker stands. A working college campus: look from the grounds and the road. Interior not checked.',
  [W('Eastern State Hospital (Kentucky)'),['Kentucky Historical Society: marker 1033, Eastern State Hospital','https://history.ky.gov/markers/eastern-state-hospital'],['Kentucky Historic Institutions: Eastern State Hospital',KYHI+'eastern-state-hospital/'],['Calhoun Construction: Newtown Administration Building renovation','https://calhounconstructs.com/?p=9461'],['Bluegrass Community and Technical College: construction projects','https://bluegrass.kctcs.edu/about/campuses/projects.aspx'],['Omni Architects: The Den at BCTC','https://omniarchitects.com/News/OmniNews_files/1dd867e20629af07653be66844e8dc1a-61.html']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('lakeland-asylum-central-state-hospital-ky','Central State Hospital site (Lakeland Asylum, Central Kentucky Lunatic Asylum)','Louisville (Lakeland)','ky','inst','gone',38.27805,-85.55445,'site','public','walk','','1873–1996',
  "Bought by the state in 1869 for a House of Reform for boys, which opened in 1871, the place was turned into Kentucky's fourth lunatic asylum in 1873 and renamed the Central Kentucky Lunatic Asylum the next year; locally it was the Lakeland Asylum, after its post office, and it was Central State Hospital from 1912. The Register form dates the core of the main building to about 1873–74, architect unknown, and says the Louisville architect John Andrewartha was consulted on its additions in 1874. The state marker says the campus grew from one brick building for 370 patients to fifteen buildings, with a working farm and orchards. A new hospital opened nearby in 1986, and the old campus was pulled down in 1996 and 1997.",
  "Nothing of the old buildings stands. When the main building and Goodwood Hall (about 1910) were put on the Register in 1983, the main block was three storeys of red brick with towers, stone stringcourses and battlements, and a columned porch. Kentucky Historic Institutions says the rubble was buried, that old barns and root cellars were kept for the park, and that the Sawyer-Hayes Community Center was built in 2009 near where the ward for Black patients stood. There are two patients' cemeteries; the site says headstones found in a creek in 1997 led to a state inquiry. Markers stand by the old entrance and the cemeteries.",
  '','The old grounds are part of E. P. "Tom" Sawyer State Park; the state marker is on the circular drive off the park entrance at 2201 Lakeland Road. Park ground: open to visitors, park hours not checked.',
  [W('Central State Hospital (Kentucky)'),NR('83002646','Central Kentucky Lunatic Asylum'),['Kentucky Historical Society: marker 2308, Lakeland Asylum','https://history.ky.gov/markers/lakeland-asylum'],['HMdb: Lakeland Asylum','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=161780'],['Kentucky Historic Institutions: Central State Hospital',KYHI+'central-state-hospital/'],KBL],
  brick='no'),
N('western-state-hospital-hopkinsville-ky','Western State Hospital (Western Lunatic Asylum)','Hopkinsville','ky','inst','standing',36.86503,-87.45129,'exact','view','drive','','1854–',
  "Kentucky's second asylum, begun in 1848 on the Spring Hill tract outside Hopkinsville, bought with money raised by the town's citizens. Kentucky Historic Institutions gives the tract as 386 acres; the state survey form says 380. The first patients came in 1854, some moved from the crowded asylum at Lexington. The main building, designed by Major N. B. Kelly, was gutted by fire in late 1860 (the dates given are November 30, 1860 and 1861); it was rebuilt between 1861 and 1867 by the builders Samuel Salter and John Orr, with two wings added. Kentucky Historic Institutions says Confederate wounded were treated, and some buried, on the grounds during the Civil War. It became Western State Hospital in 1919, held about 2,200 patients at its peak in 1950 and 1953, and still works as a state psychiatric hospital in the rebuilt Kirkbride building.",
  "The Kirkbride building, its centre block and long stepped wings, stands at the head of the grounds on Russellville Road. Wikipedia reports an inside and outside renovation in phases from 2019. What it is built of was not found in a source read.",
  '','An active state psychiatric hospital at 2400 Russellville Road (US 68). View from the public road only.',
  [W('Western State Hospital (Kentucky)'),NR('79003612','Western Lunatic Asylum'),['Kentucky Historic Institutions: Western State Hospital',KYHI+'western-state-hospital/'],KBL],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='N. B. Kelly',builder='Samuel Salter; John Orr',archw='Kentucky Historic Resources Inventory form with the Register listing: "designed and built in 1854 by Major N. B. Kelly"; rebuilt and "completed in 1867 by builders Samuel Salter and John Orr"'),
N('frankfort-state-hospital-school-cemetery-ky','Kentucky Institution for Feeble-Minded Children (Frankfort State Hospital and School) cemetery','Frankfort','ky','inst','traces',38.19341,-84.86281,'site','view','drive','','1860–1972',
  "Governor Magoffin signed the bill for the school in 1860, and Kentucky Historic Institutions calls it the second of its kind west of the Alleghenies. The state bought 61 acres at Georgetown and Glenn's Creek roads, the first pupil came in August 1860, and a four-storey building was begun on October 28, 1860. Fires followed in 1889 and the late 1890s. Overcrowded for decades, it became the Kentucky Training Home in 1945 and Frankfort State Hospital and School by 1963, with 1,129 residents, adults among them, and closed in 1972.",
  "The buildings were pulled down for a state Human Resources building; the county genealogy site says only part of the main administration building was kept, renovated as state offices on Glenn's Creek Road. Which building that is was not settled, so the pin is on the cemetery, which volunteers cleared in 2012 and 2013. It has an iron fence and flat markers, most reading \"unknown\"; the state marker, dedicated in 2014, says more than 411 people are buried there.",
  '','At the corner of Cold Harbor Drive and Glenns Creek Road, where the state marker (number 2423) stands. Look from the road; no visiting rules were found.',
  [['HMdb: Frankfort State Hospital and School Cemetery','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=84220'],['Kentucky Historic Institutions: Frankfort State Hospital and School',KYHI+'frankfort-state-hospital-school/'],['KYGenWeb Franklin County: Kentucky Feeble-Minded Institute','https://kygenweb.net/franklin/KYFeebleMindedInstitute.htm']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('kentucky-state-hospital-danville-ky','Kentucky State Hospital (Darnell General Hospital), now Northpoint Training Center','Burgin (near Danville)','ky','inst','standing',37.71021,-84.74623,'site','view','drive','','1937–1977',
  "Begun in 1937 to replace Eastern State Hospital in Lexington. Before the state could use it, it was leased in 1941 to the Army for a dollar a year and run as Darnell General Hospital, a military psychiatric hospital, which Kentucky Historic Institutions says also held German and Italian prisoners of war. The state got it back in 1946 and moved in patients from Central and Eastern State hospitals and the Frankfort school; there were 738 by February 1947. The last patients left in 1977, a youth centre used it until about 1982, and since 1983 it has been a state prison, Northpoint Training Center.",
  "Kentucky Historic Institutions says many of the original buildings are still in use inside the prison. What they look like, and who designed them, was not found.",
  '','Northpoint Training Center, a medium-security state prison at 710 Walter Reed Road. View from the public road only.',
  [['Kentucky Historic Institutions: Kentucky State Hospital',KYHI+'kentucky-state-hospital/']],
  brick='?'),
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Tennessee
N('tennessee-hospital-for-the-insane-nashville-tn','Tennessee Hospital for the Insane (Central State Hospital) site','Nashville','tn','inst','traces',36.10783,-86.6774,'area','view','drive','','1852–1995',
  "Tennessee's first asylum opened in 1840 at what is now Twelfth Avenue South and Division Street. After Dorothea Dix visited in November 1847 and urged a better one, the state bought land on Murfreesboro Pike in 1848, and Adolphus Heiman designed a Gothic hospital with octagonal towers and separate wards. It opened in 1852 (the Tennessee Encyclopedia says 1851), much of it burned in 1891, and it was renamed Central State Hospital in 1920. The hospital moved to Stewarts Ferry Pike in 1995, and the old buildings were pulled down in 1999 for a Dell computer plant.",
  "The Metro Historical Commission's marker of 2018 says a stone gatehouse and unmarked graves are all that remain. Where the gatehouse stands was not found, so the pin is on the marker.",
  '','The marker is on Murfreesboro Pike (US 41) at Dell Parkway, on the right going south. The old grounds are private business property: look from the road.',
  [W('Middle Tennessee Mental Health Institute'),['HMdb: Tennessee Hospital for the Insane','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=147132'],['Tennessee Encyclopedia: Tennessee Lunatic Asylum','https://tennesseeencyclopedia.net/?p=1724']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='Adolphus Heiman',archw='Metro Historical Commission marker: "Architect Adolphus Heiman designed" the Gothic-style complex'),
N('lyons-view-eastern-state-hospital-knoxville-tn','Lyons View Asylum (Eastern State Hospital, Lakeshore Mental Health Institute) administration building','Knoxville','tn','inst','standing',35.92239,-83.99301,'exact','public','walk','','1884–2013',
  "The Williams family gave the state 300 acres in 1874 for a mental hospital for East Tennessee. The building went up in 1884 and the hospital opened in 1886; the first patients walked more than a mile from Bearden Station, and it was long called the Lyons View Asylum. Patients worked a dairy farm and grew the kitchen's produce. A separate building for Black patients stood behind the administration building. The hospital, by then the Lakeshore Mental Health Institute, closed in 2012 or 2013 (the city and the Knoxville History Project differ), and the grounds became Lakeshore Park.",
  "The red-brick administration building on the hill was once the centre of a much longer building of wards and dormitories; most of the rest of the campus has been pulled down. The city restored it for $3.3 million in 2016, and it holds offices of the city parks department and the Lakeshore Park Conservancy. Marble Hall, a 1960s chapel, stands in the lower fields as an events pavilion.",
  '','Lakeshore Park, a City of Knoxville park; the administration building is at 5930 Lyons View Pike. The grounds are open as a park; the building is offices.',
  [['Knoxville History Project: Lakeshore Park','https://knoxvillehistoryproject.org/lakeshore-park/'],['City of Knoxville: open house at the restored building (2016)','https://www.knoxvilletn.gov/archived_news_stories/2016/ribbon_cutting___open_house_scheduled_for_sept_21_']],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes',bcol='red'),
N('western-state-hospital-bolivar-tn','Western State Hospital (Western Mental Health Institute), administration building','Bolivar','tn','inst','standing',35.27507,-89.02707,'exact','view','drive','','1886–',
  "West Tennessee's asylum, built between 1886 and 1889 to a Kirkbride plan by the brothers Harry Peake and Kenneth McDonald of Louisville, who had designed the Southwestern Lunatic Asylum at Marion, Virginia. It opened in late 1889 (November 22, says Wikipedia; December, says SAH Archipedia) with 156 patients from the crowded asylum at Nashville, and had 594 by 1900. A separate building for Black patients opened in 1897 (the Register form; Wikipedia says 1896) and was later replaced. The 1932 Polk Building was designed by Wyatt C. Hedrick. It is still a state psychiatric hospital, the county's largest employer.",
  "In 1988 the state pulled down the three-storey east and west patient wings, leaving the four-storey central tower block and its 1927 rear wing. It is Gothic Revival, of brick with corbelled brick cornices, stone sills and water table, and towers with small jerkinhead dormers; inside, SAH Archipedia notes multicoloured tile floors, pressed-tin ceilings, a turned wooden stair and a marble builder's plaque. More old buildings came down in 2008 for a new hospital that opened in 2010, and SAH Archipedia says many historic buildings stand empty.",
  '','An active state psychiatric hospital on US 64 west of Bolivar. View from the public road only.',
  [W('Western State Hospital Historic District'),NR('87001057','Western State Hospital Historic District'),['SAH Archipedia: Western Mental Health Institute','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/20239'],KBL],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='McDonald Brothers; Wyatt C. Hedrick',archw='Register record: MacDonald Bros. and Wyatt C. Hedrick; Wikipedia: the 1889 building "was designed by architect Harry Peake McDonald and his brothers", the 1932 Polk Building by Wyatt C. Hedrick'),
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- North Carolina
N('dix-hill-dorothea-dix-hospital-raleigh-nc','Dix Hill (Dorothea Dix Hospital)','Raleigh','nc','inst','standing',35.76968,-78.65597,'site','public','walk','','1856–2012',
  "North Carolina's first state asylum, which Dorothea Dix pressed for in 1848, on a hilltop that had been Theophilus Hunter's Spring Hill plantation. A. J. Davis designed it, building went on from 1850 to 1856, and the first patient came on February 22, 1856. Dix Park says it was for white patients only until 1865, when Union troops pressed it to admit Black patients; from 1880 the state sent Black patients to a separate asylum at Goldsboro. It took the name Dorothea Dix Hospital in 1959 and closed in 2012, and the grounds are now Dorothea Dix Park, Raleigh's largest city park.",
  "Davis's building was a Tuscan Revival temple-fronted centre with three-storey wings for men and women, its stuccoed brick walls scored to look like stone blocks. The centre was pulled down in 1951 or 1952 (the Register form gives both) for the six-storey McBryde Building, but the two original wings stand, restuccoed and without their first roofs, cornices and windows. The historic district round it includes the 1923 Gatekeeper's Lodge on Boylan Avenue and staff houses and ward buildings up to 1940. The patients' cemetery, begun soon after 1856, holds more than 900 graves, most unmarked.",
  '','Dorothea Dix Park, a City of Raleigh park, open as a park; the old hospital buildings were not described as open inside.',
  [W('Dorothea Dix Hospital'),W('Dix Hill'),NR('90001638','Dix Hill'),['Register form for Dix Hill (state copy)','https://files.nc.gov/ncdcr/nr/WA1847.pdf'],['City of Raleigh: Dix Hill historic district','https://raleighnc.gov/planning/services/dix-hill-historic-district'],['National Park Service: Dorothea Dix Hospital of North Carolina','https://www.nps.gov/places/dorothea-dix-hospital-of-north-carolina.htm'],['Dix Park: Gatekeeper\'s Cottage history booklet','https://dixpark.org/sites/default/files/2025-01/Gatekeepers%20Cottage%20Visitor%20Center%20History%20Exhibit%20Booklet%202024%20reduced.pdf']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',arch='A. J. Davis',archw='Register record: "Davis, A.J. et al."; Register form: the original building of 1850–1856 by A. J. Davis'),
N('broughton-hospital-avery-building-morganton-nc','Broughton Hospital (Western North Carolina Insane Asylum), Avery Building','Morganton','nc','inst','standing',35.73055,-81.67426,'site','view','drive','','1875–',
  "In 1875 the state gave $75,000 for a second asylum, for the west of the state, on 283 acres at Morganton. Samuel Sloan of Philadelphia designed the main building and James Walker of Wilmington built it; the main wing was finished late in 1882, and the Western North Carolina Insane Asylum opened on March 29, 1883. A. G. Bauer, once Sloan's assistant, drew the north wing, done in 1886. It was the State Hospital at Morganton from 1890 and Broughton Hospital from 1959, with more than 3,500 patients by the 1940s. A new hospital building on the grounds was completed in 2017.",
  "The main building, now called the Avery Building, is a big red-brick Kirkbride-plan building on its hilltop, with white keystone arches and trim, a four-storey centre under a high mansard roof and a tall dome, and stepped wings with cupolas. The 1977 Register form says the outside looks much as it did, unlike the Raleigh hospital, though most window sash is new and the inside often remodelled. Wikipedia says it is kept as offices; the UNC School of Government's reuse report saw it facing vacancy and proposed mothballing it and later a hotel or homes. The cemetery, opened in 1883, holds 1,583 marked graves.",
  '','On the grounds of Broughton Hospital, an active state psychiatric hospital, 1000 South Sterling Street. View from the public roads only.',
  [W('Broughton Hospital'),NR('77000996','Western North Carolina Insane Asylum'),['Register form: Western North Carolina Insane Asylum (state copy)','https://shpo-images.dncr.nc.gov/BK0002.pdf'],['UNC School of Government: Broughton campus reuse report','https://www.sog.unc.edu/sites/www.sog.unc.edu/files/Public-Stakeholder-Report.pdf'],KBL],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes',bcol='red',arch='Samuel Sloan; A. G. Bauer',builder='James Walker',archw='Register form: Samuel Sloan of Philadelphia architect, James Walker of Wilmington master builder, A. G. Bauer the north wing'),
N('cherry-hospital-old-campus-goldsboro-nc','Cherry Hospital old campus (Asylum for the Colored Insane)','Goldsboro','nc','inst','standing',35.39158,-78.02974,'site','view','drive','','1880–2016',
  "Built as the state's asylum for its Black citizens. The legislature chose a site in 1877, bought 171 acres two miles west of Goldsboro in 1878, and the Asylum for the Colored Insane took its first patient on August 1, 1880. For about 85 years it was the only mental hospital open to Black North Carolinians; it was renamed for Governor R. Gregg Cherry in 1959 and desegregated in 1965 under the Civil Rights Act, after which it served all races from the eastern counties. Patients moved to a new Cherry Hospital half a mile away in September 2016, and the museum in the old campus closed in January 2017.",
  "The state's history essay says most of the older buildings, among them one by A. G. Bauer, were replaced in the 1930s and 1940s; the main Woodard Building dates from 1939. The aerial still shows the old campus's long ward buildings. Two cemeteries are there, one behind the Chase Laundry Building (burials 1905 to 1928) and one behind the McFarland Building, with a memorial dedicated in 2004. Which buildings stand today was not confirmed from a source.",
  '','State-owned old campus off Stevens Mill Road, beside the new hospital; a report says both campuses are patrolled by police and security. View from the public road only. The state marker F-61 is at NC 581 and Stevens Mill Road.',
  [W('Cherry Hospital'),['North Carolina Highway Historical Marker F-61: Cherry Hospital','https://www.dncr.nc.gov/blog/2023/12/18/cherry-hospital-f-61'],['Abandoned Southeast: Cherry Hospital','https://abandonedsoutheast.com/2025/01/12/cherry-hospital/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('caswell-training-school-kinston-nc','Caswell Training School (Caswell Developmental Center)','Kinston','nc','inst','standing',35.27472,-77.61585,'area','view','drive','','1912–',
  "The General Assembly authorized a school for the \"feeble-minded\" on March 4, 1911, after Dr. Ira May Hardy lobbied for it. The cornerstone of the first building was laid on May 6, 1912, and the first fifteen residents, young women, came on July 1, 1914, living at first in a farmhouse. It was renamed the Caswell Training School in 1915, for Governor Richard Caswell. Both dormitories burned in the winter of 1918. The name changed again in 1959, 1963 and 2008, and it still serves adults with intellectual disabilities.",
  "The first building, named for Dr. Hardy, still stands in the centre of the campus, according to the state's history page; it was not picked out on the aerial, so the pin is on the campus. James A. Salter, the State Architect, designed a powerhouse in 1920, and the Benton firm drew dormitories and offices in the 1950s and 1960s. Wikipedia mentions a museum and visitors centre on the campus; its hours were not found.",
  '','An active state centre for adults with developmental disabilities at 2415 West Vernon Avenue. View from the public road; ask the centre about its museum before going.',
  [W('Caswell Developmental Center'),['NC Department of Health and Human Services: Caswell Developmental Center history','https://ncdhhs.gov/caswell-dev-ctr-history/open'],['North Carolina Architects and Builders: Caswell Training School Powerhouse','https://ncarchitects.lib.ncsu.edu/buildings/B002944']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- South Carolina
N('mills-building-sc-state-hospital-columbia-sc','South Carolina State Hospital, Mills Building (South Carolina Lunatic Asylum)','Columbia','sc','inst','standing',34.01456,-81.03329,'exact','view','walk','','1822–',
  "Authorized in 1821, the South Carolina Lunatic Asylum was one of the first public mental hospitals in the country. Robert Mills, a South Carolinian and a leader in fireproof building, designed it with rooms facing south for sun and air and with locks and hinges hidden so it would not look like a prison. The cornerstone was laid on July 22, 1822, and it was finished on December 18, 1827; the first patient came in 1828. At first it took mostly white patients; Black patients were formally admitted from 1848, and in the 1910s a separate campus for Black patients opened north of the city. Samuel Sloan added to it in 1838 and 1842. It is a National Historic Landmark.",
  "A classical building on a high basement with arcaded porticos and round-headed windows, and a spiral stair rising from the fourth floor to the cupola. The state kept it and the Jarrett Building when the rest of the Bull Street grounds were sold for the BullStreet District; Wikipedia says that in early 2026 they became the headquarters of the South Carolina Office of Resilience. A 1938 marker stands in the median of Bull Street at Elmwood Avenue.",
  '','2100 Bull Street. State offices, not open to the public (Park Service): look from the street.',
  [W('South Carolina State Hospital'),NR('70000890','South Carolina State Hospital, Mills Building'),['National Park Service: South Carolina State Hospital, Mills Building','https://www.nps.gov/places/south-carolina-state-hospital-mills-building.htm'],['South Carolina historic properties record: Mills Building','https://schpr.sc.gov/index.php/Detail/properties/S10817740004'],['HMdb: South Carolina State Hospital (1938 marker)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=37004']],
  brick='?',arch='Robert Mills; Samuel Sloan',archw='Register record: Mills, Robert; South Carolina historic properties record: designed by Robert Mills, with additions by Samuel Sloan in 1838 and 1842'),
N('babcock-building-sc-state-hospital-columbia-sc','Babcock Building, South Carolina State Hospital','Columbia','sc','inst','standing',34.01573,-81.03133,'exact','view','walk','','1858–1885',
  "The second building for patients on the State Hospital grounds, built because the Mills Building had grown too small. George E. Walker designed the first part in 1858 and died before the south wing was done; Gustavus T. Berg oversaw the north wing, and Samuel Sloan designed the centre and main entrance. Money troubles and the Civil War stretched the work over four campaigns, finished in 1885. Empty by 1994, it was sold with the BullStreet land. On September 12, 2020 a three-alarm fire brought down its dome and gutted the inside.",
  "An Italian Renaissance Revival building of about 254,000 square feet. It has since been rebuilt as 208 apartments, The Babcock, with a new dome of modern materials set on in August 2023; Historic Columbia gave the work a preservation award in 2024. Stairwells added over the years do not match the rest in style or materials. On the National Register since 1981.",
  '','2110 Pickens Street, BullStreet District. Private apartments: look from the street.',
  [W('Babcock Building, South Carolina State Hospital'),NR('81000570','Babcock Building, South Carolina State Hospital'),['Historic Columbia: 2024 preservation award, the Babcock Building','https://historiccolumbia.org/node/2494']],
  brick='?',arch='George E. Walker; Gustavus T. Berg; Samuel Sloan',archw='Register record: Walker, George E. and Sloan, Samuel, et al.; Wikipedia: first section by George E. Walker, north wing overseen by Gustavus T. Berg, centre by Samuel Sloan',
  apart='mills-building-sc-state-hospital-columbia-sc: two separate buildings of the same hospital, about 200 m apart'),
]
